    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d385">

                <pb facs="0978" n="764" sameAs="frus:frus1871/pg764"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 364.</seg></opener>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <head>Mr. Fish to General Sickles</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1871-02-01"><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >February</hi> 1, 1871.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>First point in telegram of 30th inadmissible, if construed as forbidding us to
                    question judgments in violation of international law or <pb facs="0979" n="765"
                        xml:id="pg765"/> of treaty, or if made in the absence of parties. The second
                    and third points seem to exclude naturalized citizens who have not asserted
                    their nationality before Spanish tribunals. My telegram of 28th explains our
                    views. Are these changes, which are inconsistent with telegram of 26th, made for
                    the purpose of delay? Answer, in order that instructions may be given in such
                    case. If Spanish government desire to settle in same manner as in Aspinwall
                    case, why not give Roberts full powers as in that case?</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>FISH.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
